### Mathematical Model & Formal Memory Ordering Proof

#### 1. Visibility Invariant
Let $\mathcal{V}(K) = \langle V_n, V_{n-1}, \dots, V_0 \rangle$ be the chain of versions for key $K$, where $V_n$ is the newest version. Each version $V_i$ possesses metadata $(T_{\text{create}}, T_{\text{expire}}, \text{Status})$.
For a transaction $T_{\text{txn}}$ with read snapshot $T_{\text{read}}$, a version $V_i$ is visible if and only if:
$$\text{Visible}(V_i, T_{\text{read}}) \iff \Big( V_i.T_{\text{create}} \le T_{\text{read}} \land (V_i.T_{\text{expire}} > T_{\text{read}} \lor V_i.T_{\text{expire}} = \infty) \land V_i.\text{Status} = \text{COMMITTED} \Big)$$

#### 2. Happens-Before ($\xrightarrow{hb}$) Memory Order Proof
Let $T_w$ be the writer transaction committing version $V_{\text{new}}$ at timestamp $T_{\text{commit}}$, replacing $V_{\text{old}}$:

1. **Writer Protocol**:
   * $W_1$: Write $V_{\text{new}}.\text{payload}$, $V_{\text{new}}.T_{\text{create}} \leftarrow T_{\text{commit}}$, $V_{\text{new}}.T_{\text{expire}} \leftarrow \infty$, $V_{\text{new}}.\text{Status} \leftarrow \text{COMMITTED}$.
   * $W_2$: $V_{\text{old}}.T_{\text{expire}}.\text{store}(T_{\text{commit}}, \text{std::memory_order_release})$.
   * $W_3$: Atomic CAS on Index Head / Parent Tagged Pointer:
     $$\text{Head}.\text{compare\_exchange\_strong}(V_{\text{old}}, V_{\text{new}}, \text{std::memory_order_release}, \text{std::memory_order_relaxed})$$

2. **Reader Protocol**:
   * $R_1$: Read index root / head pointer:
     $$h \leftarrow \text{Head}.\text{load}(\text{std::memory_order_acquire})$$
   * $R_2$: Traverse chain via $V.\text{PrevVersionPtr}.\text{load}(\text{std::memory_order_acquire})$.
   * $R_3$: Read $V.T_{\text{expire}}.\text{load}(\text{std::memory_order_acquire})$ and $V.T_{\text{create}}.\text{load}(\text{std::memory_order_acquire})$.

3. **Inductive Proof of Correct Visibility**:
   * By $W_3 \xrightarrow{release} R_1 \xrightarrow{acquire}$, all operations preceding $W_3$ in $T_w$ (specifically payload initialization and metadata assignment in $W_1, W_2$) **synchronize-with** the read of $h$ in $R_1$.
   * Therefore, $W_1 \xrightarrow{hb} R_1 \xrightarrow{hb} R_3$.
   * If Reader observes $h = V_{\text{new}}$, it reads $V_{\text{new}}.T_{\text{create}}$. Since $W_1 \xrightarrow{hb} R_3$, the reader is guaranteed to observe the value $T_{\text{commit}}$ without reading partially initialized memory or stale cache values.
   * If Reader observed $V_{\text{old}}$ before $W_3$, it reads $V_{\text{old}}.T_{\text{expire}}$ via $R_3$. If $W_2 \xrightarrow{hb} R_3$, it sees $T_{\text{commit}} > T_{\text{read}}$, deeming $V_{\text{old}}$ invisible if $T_{\text{read}} \ge T_{\text{commit}}$, traversing safely to the next version. If $R_3 \xrightarrow{hb} W_2$, it sees $\infty$, and since $V_{\text{old}}.T_{\text{create}} \le T_{\text{read}}$, $V_{\text{old}}$ is correctly identified as the active visible version at $T_{\text{read}}$.

$$\tag*{$\blacksquare$}$$

