### Complexity, NUMA & Hardware Architecture Analysis

#### 1. Asymptotic Complexity Table

| Operation | Time Complexity (Best / Avg) | Time Complexity (Worst / High Contention) | Space Overhead per Record |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Point Read (`Read`)** | $\mathcal{O}(\log N) + \mathcal{O}(V_{\text{visible}})$ | $\mathcal{O}(\log N) + \mathcal{O}(L_{\text{chain}})$ | $0$ dynamic allocations (pure acquire traversal) |
| **Point Write (`Write`)** | $\mathcal{O}(1)$ local buffer append | $\mathcal{O}(1)$ local buffer append | $64\text{ B}$ aligned node + heap chunk if $>16\text{ B}$ |
| **Commit Phase (`Commit`)** | $\mathcal{O}(|W| \cdot \text{CAS})$ | $\mathcal{O}(|W| \cdot K_{\text{retry}})$ | Constant ring-buffer slot in NUMA WAL |
| **Epoch Reclamation (`DEBRA`)** | $\mathcal{O}(1)$ amortized per batch | $\mathcal{O}(T_{\text{threads}})$ thread check scan | Ring amortized over $3\times$ epoch cycles |
| **Range Scan** | $\mathcal{O}(\log N + K \cdot V_{\text{avg}})$ | $\mathcal{O}(\log N + K \cdot L_{\text{max}})$ | Stack iterator (allocation-free) |

---

#### 2. Cacheline False-Sharing & Multi-Socket NUMA Mitigation

```
NUMA Node 0 (Socket 0)                          NUMA Node 1 (Socket 1)
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│ [Thread 0..31 State - 64B Aligned]   │        │ [Thread 32..63 State - 64B Aligned]  │
│ [Local RingBuffer 0 - Local DRAM]    │        │ [Local RingBuffer 1 - Local DRAM]    │
└──────────────────┬───────────────────┘        └──────────────────┬───────────────────┘
                   │                                               │
                   └─────────────── UPI / Infinity Fabric ─────────┘
                       (Read-Only Traversal & Disjoint Head Writes)
```

1. **NUMA Localized Allocations**:
   * WAL ring buffers and epoch thread structures are strictly instantiated per-NUMA domain. Threads write sequentially to local buffers, eliminating cross-socket cache line invalidations (`RFO - Request For Ownership` snoops) on the write-ahead path.
2. **Padding & Strict 64-Byte Cache Boundaries**:
   * Thread states (`ThreadState`) and tuple version headers (`TupleVersion`) are aligned to `64 bytes`. This completely avoids false sharing across adjacent array elements when cores simultaneously advance their local epoch or update distinct record versions.
3. **Atomic Contention on Version Heads**:
   * Under a Zipfian distribution ($\alpha = 0.99$), hot keys experience heavy CAS contention on the version chain head. By using `TaggedPtr`, CAS failures are detected instantly in L1D cache before modifying remote cache lines, preventing cacheline bouncing across interconnects.

---

#### 3. Concurrency Arbitration Strategy Trade-Off Matrix

```
                          Arbitration Trade-off Spectrum
    Low Contention / Reads                          High Contention Writes
  ◄────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────►
     Lock-Free CAS Chains       Hardware RTM/TSX        Central Latches
    (Selected Architecture)     (Fallback Tier)        (Degraded Fallback)
```

| Metric | Lock-Free CAS Version Chains | Transactional Lock Elision (TSX/RTM) | Centralized Latches / Ticket Arrays |
