## 4. Hardware Memory Model & Edge-Case Handling

### 4.1 Cascading Aborts & Zombie Traversals
- **Problem**: Transaction $T_w$ creates an uncommitted Version $V$ and links it to the index head. Reader $T_r$ traverses the chain while $T_w$ encounters a conflict and aborts.
- **Solution**: $V_{\text{begin}}$ remains initialized to `INF_TS` with $V_{\text{tx\_id}} = T_w$. Readers perform an `acquire` load on `begin_ts`. Any version with `begin_ts == INF_TS` is treated as uncommitted and skipped during chain traversal. Abort routines cleanly deallocate uncommitted versions without leaving dangling pointers visible to subsequent epochs.

### 4.2 Epoch Drift & Bounded Reclamation
- **Problem**: Analytical (OLAP) queries holding long-lived read snapshots prevent the global minimum active epoch from advancing, causing retirement queues to grow indefinitely.
- **Solution**: The engine tracks per-thread `min_read_ts` inside the cacheline-aligned `ThreadControlBlock`. Historical versions are pruned only if their invalidation lifespan $[V_{\text{begin}}, V_{\text{end}})$ satisfies $V_{\text{end}} \le \min_{t}(\text{active\_read\_ts}_t)$. If an OLAP query lags past a configurable threshold, the engine marks the transaction as stale and issues an explicit epoch advance warning.

### 4.3 ABA and Key Re-insertion Hazards
- **Problem**: Key $K$ is deleted (tombstone installed) and re-inserted with a new value within the same epoch cycle.
- **Solution**: Tombstones are first-class Version records with `is_tombstone = true`. Every mutation (insert, update, delete) generates a monotonically stamped Version node. Version heads are updated through CAS transitions that sequence commit timestamps, guaranteeing that re-inserts never collide with retiring historical records.

