## 1. Architecture & Formal Invariants

### 1.1 Memory Topology & Data Structure Layout

The concurrent MVCC SkipList is structured as a two-dimensional lock-free pointer mesh decoupled into an **Indexing Plane** (the SkipList tower) and a **Version Plane** (the MVCC version chains).

```
Indexing Plane (SkipList Towers with Atomic Forward Pointers):
[Head] Level 3 --------------------------------------------------------> [Node K=50] -> nullptr
       Level 2 -------------------------> [Node K=20] -----------------> [Node K=50] -> nullptr
       Level 1 ---------> [Node K=10] ---> [Node K=20] ---> [Node K=35] -> [Node K=50] -> nullptr
       Level 0 ---------> [Node K=10] ---> [Node K=20] ---> [Node K=35] -> [Node K=50] -> nullptr
                             |                |                |                |
                             v                v                v                v
Version Plane:            [V_ts=100]       [V_ts=80]        [V_ts=95]        [V_ts=120]
(Lock-free CAS               |                |                |                |
 prepended list)             v                v                v                v
                          [V_ts=45]        [TOMB_ts=50]     [V_ts=30]        [V_ts=10]
                             |                |                |                |
                             v                v                v                v
                          nullptr          [V_ts=15]        nullptr          nullptr
                                              |
                                              v
                                           nullptr
```

#### Node Structure
1. **`SkipNode<Key, Value>`**:
   * `key`: Const key instance.
   * `height`: Immutable tower height assigned at allocation.
   * `version_head`: `std::atomic<VersionNode<Value>*>` pointing to the newest MVCC version node.
   * `forward[H_max]`: Flexible/fixed array of `std::atomic<SkipNode*>`.
2. **`VersionNode<Value>`**:
   * `commit_ts`: Monotonic transaction commit timestamp (64-bit integer).
   * `is_tombstone`: Boolean flag indicating physical or logical deletion.
   * `value`: Optional payload (empty if tombstone).
   * `next_version`: `std::atomic<VersionNode<Value>*>` pointing to the predecessor version ($T_{\text{prev}} < T_{\text{curr}}$).

---

### 1.2 Formal Linearizability & Concurrency Invariants

* **Invariant 1 (Monotonic Descending Version Chain)**:
  For any version chain starting at `node->version_head`, let $V_0, V_1, \dots, V_k$ be the sequence of version nodes reachable via `next_version`. Then:
  $$\forall i \in [0, k-1], \quad V_i.\text{commit\_ts} > V_{i+1}.\text{commit\_ts}$$
  *Proof sketch*: New versions are spliced at the head via `compare_exchange_weak` only if $T_{\text{new}} > T_{\text{current\_head}}$. Out-of-order writes are rejected or serialized via transaction sequencing.

* **Invariant 2 (Snapshot Isolation Read Consistency)**:
  A reader executing with snapshot timestamp $T_{\text{read}}$ observing key $K$ traverses $V_0, V_1, \dots$ until it finds the first node $V^*$ such that $V^*.\text{commit\_ts} \le T_{\text{read}}$.
  * By Invariant 1, $V^*$ is uniquely determined and represents the most recent committed state at or before $T_{\text{read}}$.
  * Any concurrently prepended version $V_{\text{new}}$ will have $T_{\text{new}} > T_{\text{read}}$ (or will be ordered after the read linearizes), hence invisible to the snapshot reader.

* **Invariant 3 (Tower Monotonicity & Link Ordering)**:
  When linking a new `SkipNode` of height $h$:
  1. Forward pointers are initialized from Level 0 up to $h-1$ to target successors observed during optimistic search.
  2. Pointers are spliced level-by-level from Level 0 up to $h-1$ using CAS operations.
  3. Readers traversing top-down are guaranteed to land on valid keys because a node is visible at level $L > 0$ only after it has already been anchored at level $0$.

---

### 1.3 Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR) State Transitions

The memory reclamation system decouples memory retirement from physical deallocation via a global epoch counter $E_g \in \{0, 1, 2\}$ and per-thread registration descriptors.

```
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                             Thread Lifecycle                                  |
|                                                                               |
|   [ Inactive / Quiescent ]                                                    |
|              |                                                                |
|      enter() | (Sets local_epoch = global_epoch, active = true)               |
|              v                                                                |
|     [ Active Guarded ]                                                        |
|              |                                                                |
|              +---> [ Access Nodes / Read / Insert / Prepend Version ]         |
|              |                                                                |
|              +---> [ Retire Node ] ---> Added to Thread-Local Retire Queue    |
|              |                          for Global Epoch E_g                  |
|              |                                                                |
|      exit()  | (Sets active = false)                                          |
|              v                                                                |
|   [ Inactive / Quiescent ]                                                    |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         Epoch Advancement & GC Cycle                          |
|                                                                               |
|  1. Thread checks if local retired count exceeds GC_THRESHOLD.                |
|  2. Poll all registered thread descriptors:                                    |
|     If all ACTIVE threads have local_epoch == E_g:                            |
|       - E_next = (E_g + 1) mod 3                                              |
|       - CAS Global Epoch: E_g -> E_next                                       |
|  3. When Global Epoch advances to E_next:                                     |
|       - Any node retired in epoch (E_next + 1) mod 3 (i.e. two epochs ago)    |
|         is guaranteed unreachable by ANY active thread.                       |
|       - Safely physically delete/free all nodes in retired list for that epoch|
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

* **Stall Prevention**: Threads that are inactive (not currently executing an operation guard) set their `active` flag to `false`. The epoch advancement scanner ignores inactive threads, preventing idle or preempted worker threads from blocking global reclamation.

